<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Hongzhang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Hongzhang was a leading Chinese statesman and general of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>. He had a large part in suppressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> and promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>, building arsenals, industries and a modern navy. He negotiated several treaties after Qing defeats, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang.jpg" alt="Li Hongzhang" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Russell &amp; Sons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Hung_Chang_in_1896(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Hongzhang was born on 15 February 1823 into a scholar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> family in a village north-east of Hefei, in Anhui Province. His father, Li Wen'an, held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a> degree. Li obtained the juren degree in 1844, began taking classes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>, and in 1847 won the jinshi degree at the age of 24, which brought him into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanlin-academy">Hanlin Academy</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> began in January 1851, he was taken to Anhui in 1853 to help organise militias against the rebels, and was rewarded for recapturing two counties and Lu Prefecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1862 Li sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> with troops recruited at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>'s order, where they were commissioned as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-army">Huai Army</a>. He equipped it with Western firearms and artillery, and within two years its strength rose from 6,000 to about 60–70,000 men. In 1863 and 1864 it recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, Changzhou and other territories from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> rebels. An incident over the surrender of rebel leaders at Suzhou soured his relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a>, who was said to have wanted to shoot him. He later helped suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nian-rebellion">Nian Rebellion</a>.</p><p>From 1870 Li was Viceroy of Zhili and Beiyang Trade Minister, and until his death he shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>'s foreign policy to a large extent. He signed the Yantai Treaty with Sir Thomas Wade in 1876, arranged treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the French, and supported the Chinese Educational Mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He also strengthened the army and navy, fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-arthur-tasmania">Port Arthur</a> and the Dagu forts, and founded the Tianjin Military Academy in 1885. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> of 1894–1895 undermined his political standing and the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>.</p><p>Sent to negotiate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, Li was shot in the left cheek on 24 March 1895 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a> by an assassin, which led Japan to agree to a ceasefire. He signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> on 17 April 1895. In 1896 he attended the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1901 Li was the principal Chinese negotiator with the powers that had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and on 7 September he signed the Boxer Protocol. He died of liver inflammation two months later in Beijing. His reputation remains disputed. His biographer William J. Hail praised him as always progressive, yet patient and conciliatory, while Liang Qichao's 1902 biography blamed him for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s woes. History textbooks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> long called him a traitor, and a serious debate among mainland historians began only in the 1980s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Hongzhang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They also showed that the Banner and Green Standard armies could not suppress large-scale insurgency without the Han Chinese regional armies, the Xiang Army of Zeng Guofan and the Huai Army of <b>Li Hongzhang</b>, whose success shifted real military power from the Manchu court to Han regional commanders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty’s own Banner and Green Standard armies had failed against them, and the war was being won by new regional armies raised by Han Chinese scholar-officials: the Xiang Army of Zeng Guofan and the Huai Army of <b>Li Hongzhang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Against them stood Zeng Guofan, who recruited, paid and drilled village soldiers for the Xiang Army, Zuo Zongtang and <b>Li Hongzhang</b>, and the Ever Victorious Army of Frederick Townsend Ward and Charles George Gordon.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Provincial armies such as Zeng Guofan's Xiang Army and <b>Li Hongzhang's</b> Huai Army, aided by Western-officered troops, gradually recovered the Yangtze valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiping-heavenly-kingdom">Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hefei, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.86,117.28&amp;year=1873" aria-label="Hefei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7571 1004 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Li Hongzhang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8021 1274h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8021 1274h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.86,117.28&amp;year=1873">What was here in 1873? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
